\begin{document}
\title{\textblue{ Climate Predictability and  Variability in the Presence of
Anthropogenic Climate Change: \\
  A Proposal for a Princeton Climate Center
}}
\author{Geoffrey K. Vallis ~~~  Jorge Sarmiento  \\
 Isaac Held ~~~~~~~   George Philander \\   GFDL \& Princeton University }
\date{August 2000}
\maketitle


\bsli

\header{The Issues}

\benu
\item The Scientific Issue: Climate Variability and Predictability.

\item The Structural Issue: A Joint Institute between GFDL and P.U.

\eenu

\esli

\bssl
 \header {Goals and Activities}
 \bite
\item Experimental predictions (projections) of climate and climate
  change from the seasonal to the multidecadal timescales using: \benu
  \item State-of-the-art coupled ocean-atmosphere-sea-ice models.
  \item  The current state of the climate system as initial conditions.
  \item Projections of all relevant future forcing terms (GHGs, aerosols
  etc).
  \item Interactive terrestrial and marine carbon cycle.
    \eenu
 \item Quantification of climate predictability and variability.
 \item Continual development of methods for climate prediction.
 \eite

\bigskip

 \subheader{Specific Products}
\bite
 \item Periodic Reports and Assessments.
 \item Continually updated web site containing reports
     and the data from the numerical models.
 \item Archival publications in the scientific literature.
 \eite

\essl

 \bssl
 \header{Limitations to Climate Predictability}
  \benu
 \item Imperfect models;
 \item Imperfect initial and boundary conditions.
   \eenu
 Errors from these are amplified and filtered by the natural (chaotic)
 variability in the climate system.

\bigskip

 Over any given timescale:
\benu

\item What processes limit predictability?

 \item What level of predictability is achievable?

 \item How can we achieve the maximum possible predictability?

\eenu

\essl



\bssl

\header{Research Agenda}

\benu

\item \textblue{\it Understanding and quantifying climate variability.} \\
   e.g.,  Do long-term  natural oscillations (e.g., NAO, AAO) imply
    predictability? \\
    Decadal variability of ENSO. \\
    Understanding the historical record.  Paleo-variability.

\item \textblue{{\it Initialization.} } \\
 Assimilation of the state of the ocean, sea-ice
and (ultimately) land-surface. \\
 Use of Argo float program; \textit{in situ} observations; altimeter.
 \\
 Sea-ice from satellite measurements.

\item \textblue{{\it Radiative forcing and the Carbon Cycle.} } \\
   Projection of levels of external forcing agents, \\
   including GHGs, sulfates and other aerosols, \\ statistical estimates of
   volcanic eruptions.

    Interactive carbon cycle.\\
    Interaction of the terrestrial biosphere.

\item \textblue {\it Detection, attribution and prediction.} \\
      State-of-the-art models +
      statistical methodology. \\
      Differentiate between anthropogenic climate change and natural
      variability. \\
       ~\\
      ~ \hskip 1cm What aspects of the climate system is predictable? \\
       What aspects of the initial field is the future evolution sensitive to?

\eenu

\essl


\bssl
\subheader{Preliminary Experiments (partially completed)}
\bite
     \item Coupled Atmosphere-Ocean GCM.
      \item Three different oceanic initial conditions.
      \item For each oceanic initial condition, three different
      atmospheric initial conditions.
      \item Nine experiments in all.
      \eite


\subheader{Future}
\bite
\item Continually perform multiple ensembles of experiments, with
increasingly sophisticated models and increasingly accurate initial
conditions.

\item Research into the causes and nature of climate variability and
predictability, including both physical and biogeochemical processes.
\eite
\essl

\bssl
\header{Structure: A Joint Institute between GFDL and PU}

\textit{Phase 1:} Approximately 20 additional scientists and support
staff will enable the problems to be addressed.

New teams of scientists in the following areas:
\benu
\item Outreach, web-design, and interface with impacts and policy community.
\item Data Assimilation (especially ocean).
\item Statistical methodologies in prediction.
\eenu

Enhancement of existing strengths in:
\benu
\item Climate variability (e.g. `dec-cen' dynamics) and its quantification.
\item General model development (many aspects).
\item Radiative forcing and development of scenarios.
\item Carbon cycling, both terrestrial and marine.
\eenu

\essl

\bssl
\header{Some Potential Collaborations and Interactions}

\benu
\item \textit{PMEL, AOML:} Ocean measurements, incl.\ Argo.
\item
\textit{NODC:} Analysed data sets. Historical record.
\item
\textit{IRI:} Impacts. Seasonal-to-interannual (S-I) to decadal.
\item
\textit{NCEP:} S-I forecasts. Development of prediction methodology.
\item \textit{Princeton Environmental Institute, \\
Woodrow Wilson School (P.U.):}  Policy.
\eenu

\essl

\bssl

\subheader{Why?}
\bite
\item Few (if any) major efforts in the USA to these ends.
      NCAR/DoE?
      
\item There are efforts in Europe: \\
 e.g., Hadley Centre (U.K.). Max Planck
(Germany).

\item NOAA's climate services. 

\eite

\subheader{Why GFDL and Princeton University?}

\bite 

\item Combined intellectual and computational resources of GFDL
   and Princeton University.
\bite 
\item Expertise in atmospheric and oceanic modelling, and links
to  other NOAA labs and resources.

\item New model developments at GFDL.  

\item Strength in biogeochemical cycling, paleoclimate.

\item Ability to attract and train talent.
 
\eite

\item Commitment of existing personnel and resources.

\item Grand theme with breadth, excitement and focus.

\eite 

\essl

\end{document}



